# 235 · The supervisor has the CPUs to start its advisors in their own terminals

**Status:** `VERIFIED` · **Version:** [v0.1.4](../../roadmap.md#v014)

## What

On the lab, 2026-11-08, a teachers' round on a rig control set up on a cluster gave no answer at all. Its two teachers, one in Claude Code and one in Codex, started in their own terminals at the same second. Within a minute Claude Code's terminal host did not answer within its 15 seconds, and Codex had not listed its tools after 30 seconds and then exited with code 1. The supervisor's container had half a CPU, its share for a rig whose agent programs run in their own terminals. Kubernetes throttled it for 65% of its periods in that minute and 98% in the next. Its memory stayed under 320 MiB of its 4 GiB. The round an hour before had been throttled as hard at its start and had only just answered.

1. **A quarter of the rig's CPUs:** while a rig's agent programs run in their own terminals, its supervisor gets a quarter of the rig's CPUs, at least half a CPU and at most 2, and the harness the rest. A rig's least size stays 1.5 CPUs. Headless, and on a runner's pods, it keeps half a CPU.
2. **Tests:** the deployments of a rig of 16 CPUs give its supervisor 2 and its harness 14; of 6 CPUs, 1.5 and 4.5; of 1.5 CPUs, half a CPU; headless, half a CPU.

## Why

The owner wants the teachers to research every round. A round in which no teacher can start brings the run nothing, and a supervisor that only just starts them fails again on a busier minute.

## Done when

- [x] Items 1 and 2.
- [x] Pages: Add a rig on a cluster (the supervisor's share), Limits and pacing, the REST API, MCP tools, the screens' help for CPUs, the changelog.
- [x] On the lab, after the rigs' next update, the teachers' rounds start in their own terminals and answer, and the supervisor's container is throttled for less than half of its periods as they start.

## Decisions

## Progress

- 2026-11-08 14:45 UTC: built (`src/control/clusters/manifests.ts`), with the screens' and tools' words (`src/control/dashboard/rigs/`, `src/control/mcp/clusters.ts`, `src/control/clusters/resources.ts`). Tests: `tests/control/clusters/clusters.test.ts`, `tests/control/dashboard/clusters.test.ts`, `tests/control/dashboard/cluster-resources.test.ts`; they run on the lab's VM. A rig takes its new share when control next writes its deployments: an image update, a new size or a new mode.
- 2026-11-08 16:36 UTC: on the lab, with 2acab9a3. The P100 run's rig (16 CPUs) moved at 15:57 UTC, its supervisor on 2 CPUs: throttled in 15 of its first 2,150 periods (0.7%) through its first round's start, and for at most 7% of a minute (16:08 UTC) as both teachers started together in the next. The rig with runners (6 CPUs) moved at 16:07 UTC, its supervisor on 1.5 CPUs: throttled for at most 44% of a minute (16:08 UTC), a minute that also held the supervisor's own start and both teachers', and in 20% of its periods since its start. On half a CPU at 14:07 UTC it was 65%, then 98%. The tests of 2acab9a3 passed on the lab's VM, 23 of 23. In each of these rounds the Codex teacher ran for 10 s only (refused: goal 232), so the check with both teachers running past their start waits for the rigs' move onto 493c5435.
- 2026-11-08 17:18 UTC: with both teachers running past their start (the P100 run's rig on 493c5435, round of 16:58 UTC, the Codex teacher for 4 min): the supervisor on 2 CPUs was throttled for at most 4% of a minute, and in 16 of its first 5,462 periods (0.3%). The rig with runners' check with both teachers running waits for its move onto 493c5435.
- 2026-11-08 18:38 UTC: the rig with runners on 493c5435, its supervisor on 1.5 CPUs. Its round started on its own 22 s after the supervisor did (18:27:38 UTC), and both teachers ran, the Codex one for 9 min 49 s. Throttled: 51% and 52% of the first minute, which also held the supervisor's own start (49% of its first 178 periods, before the round had run 10 s); then at most 26% (18:29:30 UTC), and 1% to 6% from 18:30 UTC to the round's end; 8.3% of its periods since its start. So a round alone stays well under half on 1.5 CPUs, and only the minute after a restart, which holds the supervisor's own start as well, comes to just over half. Two CPUs for that rig, a third of its six, would clear that minute too: the owner's choice.
- 2026-11-08 20:36 UTC: **verified.** On 6d1f42ea, with the round started two minutes after the supervisor (20:26:35 UTC) on the rig with runners and its supervisor on 1.5 CPUs: throttled for at most 24% of a minute as both teachers started (20:27 UTC), 16% the next minute, then 9% or less; the supervisor's own start, apart, 4%. On the P100 run's rig, on 2 CPUs, at most 5% (20:17 UTC). Both teachers started and answered on each rig. The tests ran on the lab's VM, 23 of 23. Pages: Add a rig on a cluster (the supervisor's share), Limits and pacing, the REST API, MCP tools, the screens' help for CPUs on Add a rig and Edit rig, and the changelog's v0.1.4 section.

